[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its own table says
otherwise. The reports were merged on subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)),
and "wrong day on the invite" is what three different bugs look like from the outside.

<Callout kind="warn">
The queued parser fix closes one of the three. The other two keep coming in after it ships.
</Callout>

## What was merged

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Each row and the footnote under it is one problem, and each now has a ticket of its own.

## The three problems

**`parser`: the shared parser reads day-first dates month first.** Every tenant writes the day
first, so a booking for 7 June becomes an invite for 6 July. This is the bug #4471 was opened for:
the `parser` row. Tracked in
[furtivespy/what-it-shows#1](https://github.com/furtivespy/what-it-shows/issues/1).

**`hint`: booking-v3 tells people to type the month first, then reads the day first.** Its date
field says `MM/DD/YYYY`; the form reads the tenant's way. Only reports that came in through the
form, from tenants on booking-v3. The date is wrong before the parser ever sees it, so the parser
fix leaves these alone. This is the `hint` row, the one marked "not this ticket — no ticket of
its own". Tracked in [furtivespy/what-it-shows#2](https://github.com/furtivespy/what-it-shows/issues/2).

**One-day move: the invite lands one day off.** Tuesday 12 May shows as Monday 11 May; a client in
Lisbon gets Tuesday, a client in Toronto gets Monday. Swapping day and month cannot do that. The
desk already read these apart (`desk-notes.json`): they are the footnote under the `parser` row,
counted there by tenant. A footnote is not a ticket. The pattern points at invite dates crossing a
time zone or midnight. Tracked in
[furtivespy/what-it-shows#3](https://github.com/furtivespy/what-it-shows/issues/3).

## Lessons

- Same subject is not same cause. Merge on cause: tenant, form and channel, then the body.
- A footnote under someone else's count is a ticket nobody opened.

